WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.558 --> 00:00:08.100
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.102 --> 00:00:10.562
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.562 --> 00:00:13.094
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.096 --> 00:00:15.555
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:15.556 --> 00:00:17.950
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.952 --> 00:00:20.149
things about the world at the same

00:00:20.151 --> 00:00:21.766
time as improving your English.

00:00:22.112 --> 00:00:24.842
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it's

00:00:24.844 --> 00:00:27.332
the start of another three-part

00:00:27.332 --> 00:00:30.049
mini-series, this time on dinosaurs.

00:00:30.572 --> 00:00:32.917
In today's episode, we are going

00:00:32.919 --> 00:00:35.482
to hear the story of the Bone Wars,

00:00:35.703 --> 00:00:38.557
the bitter twenty-year feud between

00:00:38.559 --> 00:00:41.248
two American scientists that gave

00:00:41.249 --> 00:00:44.339
the world Stegosaurus, Triceratops

00:00:44.341 --> 00:00:47.156
and Diplodocus, and very nearly

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:50.093
destroyed both men in the process.

00:00:50.382 --> 00:00:52.764
In part two, we'll find out how

00:00:52.766 --> 00:00:55.222
science managed to get dinosaurs

00:00:55.404 --> 00:00:58.185
almost completely wrong for a hundred

00:00:58.187 --> 00:01:00.590
years, and how a single claw dug

00:01:00.592 --> 00:01:03.172
up in Montana changed everything.

00:01:03.455 --> 00:01:06.120
And in part three, we'll travel back

00:01:06.122 --> 00:01:08.710
sixty-six million years to the last

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:11.150
day of the dinosaurs, and follow

00:01:11.152 --> 00:01:13.743
what happened, hour by hour, after

00:01:13.744 --> 00:01:16.446
an asteroid crashed into the Earth.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.312
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:19.314 --> 00:01:22.013
minute and get right into it.

00:01:23.304 --> 00:01:26.155
If you have ever watched the hugely

00:01:26.157 --> 00:01:29.006
popular American TV series Friends,

00:01:29.392 --> 00:01:31.766
your lasting memory might be that

00:01:31.768 --> 00:01:34.069
they seem to spend a lot of time

00:01:34.361 --> 00:01:36.042
sitting around drinking coffee.

00:01:36.898 --> 00:01:39.449
Some of them, however, did have jobs.

00:01:39.977 --> 00:01:41.663
Rachel, you might remember,

00:01:41.665 --> 00:01:43.287
worked at the coffee shop.

00:01:43.645 --> 00:01:46.061
Joey had dreams of becoming an actor;

00:01:46.063 --> 00:01:48.085
he just wasn't a very good one.

00:01:48.719 --> 00:01:50.506
Monica was a chef.

00:01:51.047 --> 00:01:54.287
And Ross was a palaeontologist,

00:01:54.289 --> 00:01:56.691
an expert in dinosaurs.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.746
He was given that job, no doubt,

00:01:59.748 --> 00:02:02.060
because he was meant to be a little

00:02:02.061 --> 00:02:05.204
bit odd, a little antisocial, perhaps,

00:02:05.206 --> 00:02:07.934
and not particularly interesting.

00:02:08.613 --> 00:02:10.814
After all, unless you are five

00:02:10.816 --> 00:02:13.162
years old, the world of dinosaur

00:02:13.163 --> 00:02:15.462
discovery might seem a little…

00:02:16.224 --> 00:02:18.225
slow, uninteresting.

00:02:18.812 --> 00:02:21.015
You are dealing with objects from

00:02:21.017 --> 00:02:23.284
millions of years ago, dusting off

00:02:23.456 --> 00:02:26.516
bones, filing away things in drawers.

00:02:27.038 --> 00:02:28.894
With all due respect to any

00:02:28.896 --> 00:02:31.574
palaeontologists who might be listening

00:02:31.575 --> 00:02:34.392
to this, it's possible to think that

00:02:34.394 --> 00:02:37.209
dinosaurs are fascinating but to not

00:02:37.228 --> 00:02:39.518
want to spend your life studying them.

00:02:40.612 --> 00:02:43.278
The early years of palaeontology,

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.864
however, were anything but dull.

00:02:46.524 --> 00:02:49.467
They were shaped by a great rivalry, the

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:52.263
greatest rivalry in the history of the

00:02:52.263 --> 00:02:55.525
subject, and perhaps one of the greatest

00:02:55.527 --> 00:02:58.461
rivalries in the history of science.

00:02:59.257 --> 00:03:01.793
It has been called the Bone Wars.

00:03:02.164 --> 00:03:06.564
It ran for more than two decades, and it

00:03:06.566 --> 00:03:10.965
involved bribery, spying, theft, smashed

00:03:10.989 --> 00:03:14.469
fossils, and a full-scale public scandal

00:03:14.471 --> 00:03:18.124
on the front page of a New York newspaper.

00:03:18.855 --> 00:03:21.890
It ruined the two men at the centre of it.

00:03:22.489 --> 00:03:24.638
It also, almost by accident,

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:26.864
gave the world the dinosaurs.

00:03:27.739 --> 00:03:30.997
And like many a great rivalry,

00:03:30.999 --> 00:03:34.690
the rivals started out as friends.

00:03:35.911 --> 00:03:39.718
The first of them, Edward Drinker Cope,

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.428
was born in Philadelphia in 1840, into

00:03:43.428 --> 00:03:46.570
a wealthy family of Quakers, members of

00:03:46.572 --> 00:03:49.712
a quiet and strict Christian community.

00:03:50.509 --> 00:03:53.116
He was a child prodigy.

00:03:53.707 --> 00:03:56.209
He was studying anatomy as a boy,

00:03:56.211 --> 00:03:58.712
publishing scientific papers as a

00:03:58.712 --> 00:04:01.665
teenager, and by his early twenties he

00:04:01.667 --> 00:04:04.696
had a reputation as one of the sharpest

00:04:04.696 --> 00:04:07.287
minds in American natural history.

00:04:08.008 --> 00:04:10.596
He was also, by all accounts, a

00:04:10.598 --> 00:04:13.768
difficult man: he was charming, but he

00:04:13.768 --> 00:04:16.918
was also hot-tempered, and completely

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:20.239
incapable of backing down from a fight.

00:04:21.392 --> 00:04:24.094
His rival was a man with the

00:04:24.096 --> 00:04:27.471
magnificent name of Othniel Charles

00:04:27.501 --> 00:04:30.767
Marsh, and he was almost the opposite.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:35.182
He was born nine years earlier, in 1831,

00:04:35.184 --> 00:04:38.632
on a poor farm in upstate New York.

00:04:39.283 --> 00:04:41.869
His mother died when he was a small boy.

00:04:42.421 --> 00:04:44.949
There was no money and, for a

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:47.478
long time, no obvious future.

00:04:48.115 --> 00:04:52.420
But Marsh had one great piece of luck:

00:04:52.422 --> 00:04:56.158
his uncle was a man called George

00:04:56.274 --> 00:04:59.551
Peabody, who just so happened to be one

00:04:59.553 --> 00:05:02.576
of the richest bankers in the world.

00:05:03.791 --> 00:05:07.122
Peabody paid for his nephew's education,

00:05:07.124 --> 00:05:09.621
first at school, then at Yale.

00:05:10.903 --> 00:05:14.503
And Marsh, who was slow, methodical and

00:05:14.505 --> 00:05:18.104
extremely patient, made the most of it.

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:20.587
He studied hard, and became the

00:05:20.589 --> 00:05:22.534
first professor of palaeontology

00:05:22.834 --> 00:05:23.935
in the United States.

00:05:24.816 --> 00:05:27.894
And palaeontology, by the way, isn't only

00:05:27.896 --> 00:05:30.747
about dinosaurs; it's simply the study

00:05:30.771 --> 00:05:33.556
of ancient life through fossils, the most

00:05:33.558 --> 00:05:36.408
interesting of which tend to be dinosaurs.

00:05:37.524 --> 00:05:40.572
And although the subject matter is,

00:05:40.574 --> 00:05:43.881
by definition, ancient, the discipline

00:05:43.991 --> 00:05:47.593
isn't; in the 1860s, when this was all

00:05:47.595 --> 00:05:51.480
taking place, it was a brand new science.

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:55.077
Darwin's On the Origin of Species had

00:05:55.079 --> 00:05:58.023
been published in 1859, which meant that

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:01.321
suddenly every fossil became potential

00:06:01.323 --> 00:06:04.093
evidence, evidence of evolution.

00:06:04.876 --> 00:06:08.039
So, we have Edward Cope and Othniel

00:06:08.041 --> 00:06:11.563
Marsh, two great young palaeontologists

00:06:11.827 --> 00:06:13.995
cutting their teeth in the very

00:06:13.997 --> 00:06:15.954
first days of palaeontology.

00:06:16.544 --> 00:06:18.663
And as you heard, before they

00:06:18.665 --> 00:06:20.929
were rivals, they were friends.

00:06:21.669 --> 00:06:25.717
The friendship began in Berlin, in 1864.

00:06:26.274 --> 00:06:28.761
Cope was twenty-three, touring the great

00:06:28.763 --> 00:06:30.938
museums and universities of Europe.

00:06:31.381 --> 00:06:33.213
Marsh was thirty-two, and

00:06:33.215 --> 00:06:34.826
finishing his studies.

00:06:35.489 --> 00:06:38.649
They were young, a long way from home,

00:06:38.651 --> 00:06:42.142
and American palaeontologists were few and

00:06:42.154 --> 00:06:44.504
far between; there weren't many of them.

00:06:45.226 --> 00:06:48.324
They went to museums together, walked

00:06:48.326 --> 00:06:51.087
and talked, and exchanged letters

00:06:51.125 --> 00:06:53.143
and photographs when they parted.

00:06:54.325 --> 00:06:57.430
When they got home, they even named newly

00:06:57.432 --> 00:07:00.610
discovered species after each other, which

00:07:00.903 --> 00:07:03.423
is kind of cute, almost flirty, even.

00:07:04.367 --> 00:07:06.324
So what went wrong?

00:07:07.403 --> 00:07:10.773
The first crack came in 1868,

00:07:10.775 --> 00:07:14.142
in a muddy pit in New Jersey.

00:07:15.263 --> 00:07:17.947
Cope was working the marl pits of

00:07:17.949 --> 00:07:21.118
Haddonfield, quarries near Philadelphia

00:07:21.305 --> 00:07:23.805
where workers digging fertiliser

00:07:23.807 --> 00:07:26.149
kept turning up ancient bones.

00:07:27.157 --> 00:07:29.383
This was sacred ground for

00:07:29.385 --> 00:07:31.352
American palaeontology.

00:07:31.987 --> 00:07:33.844
A decade earlier, one of the

00:07:33.846 --> 00:07:35.833
first nearly complete dinosaur

00:07:35.837 --> 00:07:38.587
skeletons ever found, Hadrosaurus,

00:07:38.589 --> 00:07:41.095
had been dug out of these pits.

00:07:42.632 --> 00:07:45.937
Cope generously invited his friend Marsh

00:07:45.939 --> 00:07:48.995
down from Yale and showed him around.

00:07:49.752 --> 00:07:52.693
Marsh smiled, admired the fossils,

00:07:52.695 --> 00:07:55.461
thanked his host, and went home.

00:07:56.258 --> 00:07:58.735
But whether down there in the

00:07:58.737 --> 00:08:01.554
pit, or dreamed up months before,

00:08:02.016 --> 00:08:04.794
he had hatched a dastardly plan.

00:08:05.715 --> 00:08:08.505
He secretly paid the pit operators

00:08:08.507 --> 00:08:11.131
to send their future discoveries

00:08:11.711 --> 00:08:15.282
not to Cope, but to him, at Yale.

00:08:16.902 --> 00:08:20.728
When Cope found out, he was furious.

00:08:21.683 --> 00:08:23.607
Then came the affair of

00:08:23.609 --> 00:08:25.783
the backwards sea monster.

00:08:26.868 --> 00:08:29.988
In March of 1868, a large wooden

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:33.011
crate arrived at the Academy of

00:08:33.011 --> 00:08:35.410
Natural Sciences in Philadelphia.

00:08:36.644 --> 00:08:38.596
Inside it were bones.

00:08:39.002 --> 00:08:41.888
Huge, strange bones, dug out of

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:44.774
the rocks of western Kansas and

00:08:44.775 --> 00:08:46.860
sent east by an army doctor.

00:08:47.958 --> 00:08:51.442
Cope opened the crate and got to work.

00:08:52.128 --> 00:08:55.022
He laid out the bones, studied them,

00:08:55.024 --> 00:08:57.996
and pieced together a creature unlike

00:08:58.095 --> 00:09:01.649
anything anyone had ever seen: a giant

00:09:01.651 --> 00:09:05.015
sea reptile with a short neck and an

00:09:05.015 --> 00:09:08.536
extraordinarily long, snake-like tail.

00:09:09.978 --> 00:09:12.220
He named it Elasmosaurus.

00:09:12.751 --> 00:09:14.985
He presented it to his colleagues,

00:09:14.987 --> 00:09:17.021
published a paper, and included

00:09:17.233 --> 00:09:19.153
a beautiful illustration of

00:09:19.155 --> 00:09:21.073
the reconstructed skeleton.

00:09:22.173 --> 00:09:24.692
There was just one problem, a problem

00:09:24.694 --> 00:09:26.938
you might be familiar with if you

00:09:26.939 --> 00:09:28.982
are the sort of person who doesn't

00:09:28.984 --> 00:09:31.145
properly read the IKEA instructions.

00:09:32.059 --> 00:09:35.306
Cope put the head on the wrong end.

00:09:36.209 --> 00:09:38.791
The real animal had a long,

00:09:38.793 --> 00:09:41.852
swan-like neck and a short tail.

00:09:42.501 --> 00:09:45.524
Cope had rebuilt it backwards,

00:09:45.526 --> 00:09:48.850
and mistaken the neck for a tail.

00:09:49.959 --> 00:09:52.308
The mistake was pointed out publicly

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:54.396
two years later, at a scientific

00:09:54.396 --> 00:09:56.313
meeting, by an older professor.

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:58.713
It was done politely, as these

00:09:58.715 --> 00:10:00.241
things tended to be done.

00:10:00.966 --> 00:10:03.436
After all, a neck does look a bit

00:10:03.438 --> 00:10:05.906
like a tail, and putting the bits

00:10:05.906 --> 00:10:07.911
of a puzzle together is harder still

00:10:07.913 --> 00:10:09.637
if you don't even know what the

00:10:09.637 --> 00:10:11.123
final thing is meant to look like.

00:10:12.195 --> 00:10:15.629
Cope, understandably, was mortified.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:18.489
He tried to buy back every printed

00:10:18.491 --> 00:10:20.784
copy of his paper so that the error

00:10:20.785 --> 00:10:22.551
would disappear from the record.

00:10:23.515 --> 00:10:26.271
He managed to buy back all but one.

00:10:26.993 --> 00:10:29.959
One copy remained in the hands of his

00:10:29.961 --> 00:10:32.926
former friend, Othniel Charles Marsh.

00:10:33.746 --> 00:10:36.727
He kept it, and he would retell the story

00:10:36.729 --> 00:10:39.636
of the backwards sea monster, with great

00:10:39.674 --> 00:10:42.109
pleasure, for the next thirty years.

00:10:43.389 --> 00:10:45.379
By the early 1870s, the

00:10:45.381 --> 00:10:47.110
friendship was dead.

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:50.867
What replaced it was a competition

00:10:50.869 --> 00:10:54.205
unlike anything science had seen before.

00:10:55.114 --> 00:10:57.962
The battlefield was the American West.

00:10:59.076 --> 00:11:01.271
The first railway across the American

00:11:01.273 --> 00:11:03.288
continent had just been completed,

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:06.044
and it cut straight through some of

00:11:06.046 --> 00:11:08.537
the richest fossil country on Earth:

00:11:08.828 --> 00:11:12.546
Kansas, Wyoming, Colorado, and Nebraska.

00:11:13.438 --> 00:11:15.675
Railway cuttings and eroded hillsides

00:11:15.677 --> 00:11:18.215
were spilling out the bones of these great

00:11:18.216 --> 00:11:20.837
creatures nobody had ever studied before.

00:11:21.784 --> 00:11:24.080
For a palaeontologist,

00:11:24.082 --> 00:11:26.271
this was a gold rush.

00:11:26.943 --> 00:11:29.800
And Cope and Marsh raced each other

00:11:29.802 --> 00:11:32.330
into it like rival prospectors.

00:11:33.465 --> 00:11:35.397
It was not gentle work.

00:11:35.937 --> 00:11:38.238
The fossil country was dry,

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.051
empty land, blazing hot in summer

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:43.295
and bitterly cold in winter.

00:11:44.489 --> 00:11:48.069
Much of it was badlands: bare, broken,

00:11:48.071 --> 00:11:52.025
eroded country where almost nothing grows.

00:11:53.189 --> 00:11:55.891
Collectors worked there with picks

00:11:55.893 --> 00:11:58.196
and shovels, the nearest town

00:11:58.395 --> 00:12:00.518
sometimes days away by wagon.

00:12:01.311 --> 00:12:04.616
They drank foul water, breathed bitter

00:12:04.618 --> 00:12:07.835
mineral dust, and slept under canvas.

00:12:08.890 --> 00:12:13.680
Then, in 1877, everything exploded.

00:12:14.559 --> 00:12:18.214
That year, a schoolteacher in Colorado

00:12:18.216 --> 00:12:21.388
named Arthur Lakes wrote to Marsh

00:12:21.594 --> 00:12:23.878
about some enormous bones he had

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:26.233
found sticking out of a hillside.

00:12:27.297 --> 00:12:30.002
When Marsh was slow to reply,

00:12:30.004 --> 00:12:32.613
Lakes wrote to Cope as well.

00:12:33.548 --> 00:12:35.784
That got Marsh's attention.

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:39.232
He sent money immediately, hired Lakes,

00:12:39.234 --> 00:12:41.895
and told him to keep everything secret.

00:12:43.278 --> 00:12:46.436
A few months later, in the summer

00:12:46.438 --> 00:12:49.402
of 1877, Marsh received another

00:12:49.403 --> 00:12:52.287
letter, this time from Laramie,

00:12:52.289 --> 00:12:55.265
a small railway town in Wyoming.

00:12:56.551 --> 00:12:59.028
It came from two men who worked

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:01.106
on the railway at a lonely

00:13:01.118 --> 00:13:02.817
little station called Como.

00:13:04.024 --> 00:13:06.386
They had found a large number of

00:13:06.388 --> 00:13:08.748
fossils, they said, some of them

00:13:08.775 --> 00:13:12.324
enormous, not far from where they lived.

00:13:13.202 --> 00:13:15.184
But they were very careful

00:13:15.186 --> 00:13:17.165
about what they gave away.

00:13:18.090 --> 00:13:20.142
They signed the letter with their

00:13:20.144 --> 00:13:22.194
middle names, Harlow and Edwards,

00:13:22.526 --> 00:13:24.036
instead of their real ones.

00:13:24.674 --> 00:13:27.399
They posted it from Laramie, so that

00:13:27.401 --> 00:13:30.200
the postmark would not point to Como.

00:13:31.009 --> 00:13:32.836
And they said nothing about

00:13:32.838 --> 00:13:34.867
where the bones actually were.

00:13:35.708 --> 00:13:38.267
What they were offering, they explained,

00:13:38.269 --> 00:13:40.250
was to sell Marsh their fossils

00:13:40.501 --> 00:13:42.528
“and also the secret of the others”.

00:13:43.194 --> 00:13:44.642
They were working men, they

00:13:44.644 --> 00:13:46.358
wrote, and they could not afford

00:13:46.358 --> 00:13:47.904
to give them away as a gift.

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.398
Marsh, on the other hand, was well

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:54.357
known as “a man of means”, a rich man.

00:13:55.336 --> 00:13:57.350
The location, in other words,

00:13:57.352 --> 00:13:58.947
was the thing for sale.

00:13:59.406 --> 00:14:01.498
If they told Marsh where the bones

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:03.651
were, there was nothing to stop him

00:14:03.667 --> 00:14:05.891
sending his own men to dig them up, and

00:14:05.893 --> 00:14:08.058
paying the two of them nothing at all.

00:14:09.058 --> 00:14:10.962
They also mentioned, helpfully,

00:14:10.964 --> 00:14:12.744
that other people in the area

00:14:12.749 --> 00:14:15.020
were “looking for such things”.

00:14:16.370 --> 00:14:18.209
Marsh did not need to be

00:14:18.211 --> 00:14:19.971
told who that might be.

00:14:20.736 --> 00:14:22.885
He sent them a cheque immediately.

00:14:23.297 --> 00:14:25.090
But their anonymity

00:14:25.092 --> 00:14:27.261
came back to bite them.

00:14:27.838 --> 00:14:30.344
The cheque was made out to Harlow

00:14:30.346 --> 00:14:32.774
and Edwards, their middle names,

00:14:33.057 --> 00:14:34.981
so the men could not cash it.

00:14:36.382 --> 00:14:38.641
Marsh then sent one of his

00:14:38.643 --> 00:14:41.074
assistants out to Wyoming to

00:14:41.194 --> 00:14:42.962
check whether the story was true.

00:14:44.023 --> 00:14:44.631
It was.

00:14:45.726 --> 00:14:48.647
The bones ran along a long, low

00:14:48.649 --> 00:14:51.568
ridge called Como Bluff, and it

00:14:51.568 --> 00:14:54.412
turned out to be one of the greatest

00:14:54.414 --> 00:14:56.861
dinosaur sites ever discovered.

00:14:58.017 --> 00:15:00.634
Marsh signed the two men up on a

00:15:00.636 --> 00:15:03.905
monthly wage, with bonuses for important

00:15:03.914 --> 00:15:07.066
finds, and told them to keep Cope out.

00:15:08.031 --> 00:15:09.567
They were right to be careful.

00:15:10.208 --> 00:15:12.856
Within two years, Cope's spies

00:15:12.858 --> 00:15:15.593
had found out about Como Bluff,

00:15:15.978 --> 00:15:18.195
and Cope's collectors had set

00:15:18.197 --> 00:15:20.488
up camp practically next door.

00:15:21.713 --> 00:15:24.213
One of Marsh's own men, unhappy

00:15:24.215 --> 00:15:26.794
with his pay, switched sides and

00:15:26.795 --> 00:15:28.495
started digging for the enemy.

00:15:29.709 --> 00:15:33.053
What followed was less like science

00:15:33.055 --> 00:15:36.397
and more like a small, strange war.

00:15:36.994 --> 00:15:39.644
Each man built a network of field

00:15:39.646 --> 00:15:42.295
collectors, spies and informants.

00:15:42.819 --> 00:15:45.122
Telegrams were sent in code, so

00:15:45.124 --> 00:15:47.350
that rival agents reading them

00:15:47.574 --> 00:15:49.534
would not learn what had been found.

00:15:50.137 --> 00:15:52.243
Workers were bribed to reveal

00:15:52.245 --> 00:15:54.059
secrets, or to keep them.

00:15:54.713 --> 00:15:57.481
Crews spied on each other's quarries, and

00:15:57.483 --> 00:16:00.182
occasionally threw stones at each other.

00:16:01.401 --> 00:16:04.615
The digging did not even stop for winter.

00:16:05.448 --> 00:16:07.959
At Como Bluff, Marsh's men kept

00:16:07.961 --> 00:16:10.714
working through the bitter Wyoming

00:16:10.727 --> 00:16:13.021
blizzards, hacking bones out of

00:16:13.023 --> 00:16:15.537
frozen rock, because a quarry left

00:16:15.627 --> 00:16:18.215
unattended, well, it was unattended,

00:16:18.217 --> 00:16:20.587
and fair game for the other side.

00:16:21.743 --> 00:16:24.821
And when a digging season ended for good,

00:16:24.823 --> 00:16:27.898
some crews would fill their quarries back

00:16:27.898 --> 00:16:30.750
in with earth and rock, or even, it was

00:16:30.752 --> 00:16:33.529
rumoured, smash the smaller bones that

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:35.865
remained, rather than leave anything

00:16:35.867 --> 00:16:38.525
behind for their dinosaur-digging rivals.

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:42.417
Back east, the war was fought

00:16:42.419 --> 00:16:44.460
in scientific journals.

00:16:45.282 --> 00:16:49.181
In the 1870s and 1880s, the two men were

00:16:49.183 --> 00:16:52.983
publishing at a furious pace, racing to

00:16:52.983 --> 00:16:55.537
be the first to name each new species.

00:16:56.336 --> 00:16:58.339
The rules of science, after all,

00:16:58.341 --> 00:17:00.279
are quite simple as far as this

00:17:00.279 --> 00:17:03.299
is concerned: whoever publishes a

00:17:03.301 --> 00:17:06.228
valid name first, wins, and that

00:17:06.228 --> 00:17:07.678
becomes the name of the creature.

00:17:08.523 --> 00:17:11.114
So speed was everything.

00:17:11.908 --> 00:17:15.018
Cope in particular would rush descriptions

00:17:15.020 --> 00:17:17.980
into print within days, sometimes within

00:17:18.085 --> 00:17:20.372
hours, of a crate of bones arriving.

00:17:21.286 --> 00:17:23.421
Marsh accused him of writing

00:17:23.423 --> 00:17:25.175
false, earlier dates on

00:17:25.175 --> 00:17:26.943
his papers to win the race.

00:17:27.616 --> 00:17:29.968
Cope accused Marsh of everything

00:17:29.970 --> 00:17:32.614
from plagiarism to scientific fraud.

00:17:33.776 --> 00:17:36.023
The pace produced chaos.

00:17:36.897 --> 00:17:39.961
Between them, the two men named

00:17:39.963 --> 00:17:43.321
more than 140 species of dinosaur.

00:17:44.302 --> 00:17:46.640
Many of these later turned out

00:17:46.642 --> 00:17:49.211
to be duplicates, the same animal

00:17:49.211 --> 00:17:51.193
named twice, or three times,

00:17:51.195 --> 00:17:53.316
from different piles of bones.

00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:58.949
In 1877, for example, Marsh named a huge

00:17:58.951 --> 00:18:02.900
long-necked plant-eater Apatosaurus.

00:18:03.982 --> 00:18:06.324
Two years later, working from another

00:18:06.326 --> 00:18:08.223
skeleton, he named another one

00:18:08.476 --> 00:18:10.988
Brontosaurus, the "thunder lizard".

00:18:11.788 --> 00:18:13.930
They were, it later emerged,

00:18:13.932 --> 00:18:16.379
essentially the same animal, and

00:18:16.551 --> 00:18:18.871
the name Brontosaurus was officially

00:18:18.873 --> 00:18:20.804
retired for most of a century.

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:23.519
Though, in a nice twist, some

00:18:23.521 --> 00:18:25.546
modern palaeontologists have

00:18:25.547 --> 00:18:27.157
argued for bringing it back.

00:18:28.578 --> 00:18:31.464
Cope, for his part, found a way to

00:18:31.466 --> 00:18:34.605
weaponise even the naming of species.

00:18:35.603 --> 00:18:38.556
In 1884, he named a small fossil

00:18:38.558 --> 00:18:41.047
mammal Anisonchus cophater.

00:18:42.328 --> 00:18:44.318
A colleague asked him about the

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:46.629
Greek origin of the word "cophater".

00:18:47.298 --> 00:18:48.734
Cope cheerfully explained

00:18:48.736 --> 00:18:50.400
that it was not Greek at all.

00:18:50.707 --> 00:18:53.065
It was simply "Cope-hater",

00:18:53.067 --> 00:18:55.684
in honour, he said, of all the

00:18:55.758 --> 00:18:57.783
Cope-haters who surrounded him.

00:18:58.425 --> 00:19:00.539
And at the top of that list, of

00:19:00.541 --> 00:19:02.857
course, was Othniel Charles Marsh.

00:19:03.691 --> 00:19:06.127
And to think, these were men who had

00:19:06.129 --> 00:19:08.428
previously named discoveries after

00:19:08.461 --> 00:19:10.939
each other out of mutual admiration.

00:19:12.092 --> 00:19:14.585
But strip away the pettiness,

00:19:14.587 --> 00:19:16.992
and the scientific haul from

00:19:16.992 --> 00:19:18.978
these years is enormous.

00:19:19.990 --> 00:19:22.146
From Marsh's quarries came

00:19:22.148 --> 00:19:24.966
Stegosaurus, with its plated back.

00:19:25.398 --> 00:19:26.946
Allosaurus, the great

00:19:26.948 --> 00:19:28.788
predator of the Jurassic.

00:19:29.144 --> 00:19:31.198
Diplodocus, one of the longest

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:33.320
animals ever to walk the Earth.

00:19:34.204 --> 00:19:37.075
And, near the end, Triceratops, the

00:19:37.077 --> 00:19:40.110
three-horned giant that has decorated

00:19:40.135 --> 00:19:41.956
children's bedrooms ever since.

00:19:42.976 --> 00:19:45.246
Cope named the Camarasaurus, the

00:19:45.248 --> 00:19:47.586
most common giant of the American

00:19:47.587 --> 00:19:50.037
Jurassic, and Coelophysis, one of

00:19:50.039 --> 00:19:52.561
the earliest dinosaurs ever known.

00:19:53.541 --> 00:19:56.226
He described hundreds of other ancient

00:19:56.228 --> 00:19:58.840
creatures, from fossil fish to flying

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:01.714
reptiles, in a lifetime output of

00:20:01.716 --> 00:20:05.197
around 1,400 scientific papers, a number

00:20:05.509 --> 00:20:07.575
that has probably never been matched.

00:20:08.441 --> 00:20:10.771
Museums on the east coast filled

00:20:10.773 --> 00:20:13.247
up, crate by crate, with creatures

00:20:13.473 --> 00:20:15.947
no human being had ever imagined.

00:20:16.754 --> 00:20:18.678
Newspapers picked up the story.

00:20:19.237 --> 00:20:21.436
The public, for the first time,

00:20:21.438 --> 00:20:23.423
fell in love with dinosaurs.

00:20:24.337 --> 00:20:27.069
But wars are expensive, and

00:20:27.071 --> 00:20:30.307
this one was consuming both men.

00:20:31.529 --> 00:20:34.309
Cope had inherited a fortune, and

00:20:34.311 --> 00:20:37.173
he spent essentially all of it: on

00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:39.904
collectors, on shipments, on buying

00:20:39.906 --> 00:20:42.198
a scientific journal to guarantee

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:44.134
himself somewhere to publish quickly.

00:20:45.035 --> 00:20:47.092
What remained, he invested

00:20:47.094 --> 00:20:49.307
in silver mines that failed.

00:20:50.687 --> 00:20:53.295
By the late 1880s, this man from

00:20:53.297 --> 00:20:55.904
one of Philadelphia's wealthiest

00:20:56.064 --> 00:20:58.537
families was renting out his house

00:20:58.539 --> 00:21:01.082
and living among his fossil crates,

00:21:01.557 --> 00:21:04.480
low on money and low on friends.

00:21:05.468 --> 00:21:07.683
Marsh had the safer position.

00:21:08.118 --> 00:21:11.548
He had Yale, the Peabody money, and

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:14.880
from 1882 a powerful official post

00:21:15.151 --> 00:21:17.218
as chief palaeontologist of the

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:19.352
United States Geological Survey.

00:21:20.038 --> 00:21:22.530
So Cope, broke and bitter,

00:21:22.532 --> 00:21:25.214
decided that if he was going

00:21:25.226 --> 00:21:27.834
down, Marsh was coming with him.

00:21:29.086 --> 00:21:32.297
He had spent years keeping a journal of

00:21:32.299 --> 00:21:35.590
every accusation, every rumour and every

00:21:35.592 --> 00:21:38.681
complaint against Marsh: from underpaid

00:21:38.683 --> 00:21:41.611
field workers, from junior scientists

00:21:41.761 --> 00:21:44.063
whose work Marsh had published under

00:21:44.065 --> 00:21:46.621
his own name, from anyone with a grudge.

00:21:47.443 --> 00:21:49.461
He handed the whole file to a

00:21:49.463 --> 00:21:51.826
journalist at the New York Herald.

00:21:53.215 --> 00:21:55.791
On the 12th of January 1890,

00:21:55.793 --> 00:21:58.643
the story ran with the headline

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:01.974
"Scientists Wage Bitter Warfare".

00:22:03.103 --> 00:22:05.133
It was, by all accounts, a badly

00:22:05.135 --> 00:22:07.480
researched and badly written article.

00:22:08.109 --> 00:22:10.297
Still, it was a sensation.

00:22:11.132 --> 00:22:13.921
America's two most famous scientists were

00:22:13.923 --> 00:22:16.506
suddenly accusing each other of theft,

00:22:16.851 --> 00:22:19.818
fraud and incompetence in the pages of

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:22.628
a national newspaper, day after day,

00:22:22.917 --> 00:22:25.665
like two men brawling in the street.

00:22:27.002 --> 00:22:27.990
Nobody won.

00:22:28.919 --> 00:22:31.358
Cope gained little except the

00:22:31.360 --> 00:22:33.966
satisfaction of a public fight.

00:22:34.829 --> 00:22:36.860
Marsh was wounded badly.

00:22:37.519 --> 00:22:40.293
The scandal drew attention to his

00:22:40.295 --> 00:22:43.488
government position, and in 1892, when

00:22:43.490 --> 00:22:45.952
Congress was looking for budgets to

00:22:45.954 --> 00:22:48.485
cut, his funding was an easy target.

00:22:49.169 --> 00:22:50.679
He was forced to resign.

00:22:51.982 --> 00:22:54.396
The last years of both men

00:22:54.398 --> 00:22:56.811
were quiet and rather sad.

00:22:57.450 --> 00:22:59.721
And they never buried the hatchet:

00:22:59.723 --> 00:23:02.126
they never apologised to each other.

00:23:03.148 --> 00:23:06.044
Cope sold off a large part of his beloved

00:23:06.046 --> 00:23:08.870
fossil collection to the American Museum

00:23:08.887 --> 00:23:11.015
of Natural History to pay his bills.

00:23:12.199 --> 00:23:15.211
He died in 1897, at the age of

00:23:15.213 --> 00:23:18.524
fifty-six, in a room stacked with

00:23:18.525 --> 00:23:21.129
fossil crates, sleeping on a camp

00:23:21.131 --> 00:23:23.655
bed surrounded by his specimens.

00:23:24.658 --> 00:23:28.050
Even in death, he could not quite let go.

00:23:28.843 --> 00:23:30.989
He donated his body to science,

00:23:30.991 --> 00:23:32.788
and the story goes that he

00:23:32.789 --> 00:23:35.127
wanted his skull to be measured.

00:23:36.088 --> 00:23:39.198
Brain size, at the time, was believed

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:41.971
to be a mark of intelligence, and

00:23:42.010 --> 00:23:44.793
Cope expected Marsh to accept the

00:23:44.795 --> 00:23:47.828
same challenge, skull against skull.

00:23:48.852 --> 00:23:50.146
Marsh did not.

00:23:50.741 --> 00:23:53.776
He died two years later, in 1899.

00:23:54.339 --> 00:23:56.713
The man who had spent a banker's

00:23:56.715 --> 00:23:59.533
fortune on bones had but a few hundred

00:23:59.538 --> 00:24:01.848
dollars in the bank, a mortgaged house,

00:24:01.850 --> 00:24:04.039
and the greatest fossil collection in

00:24:04.039 --> 00:24:07.146
America, which he duly left to Yale.

00:24:08.305 --> 00:24:11.639
But despite the somewhat unglamorous end

00:24:11.641 --> 00:24:14.806
for both of its participants, the Bone

00:24:14.811 --> 00:24:17.649
Wars was a hugely productive period in

00:24:17.651 --> 00:24:20.637
terms of our understanding of dinosaurs.

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:24.671
When Cope and Marsh started work,

00:24:24.673 --> 00:24:28.103
science knew of nine species of dinosaur

00:24:28.203 --> 00:24:30.683
from the whole of North America, most

00:24:30.685 --> 00:24:32.961
of them from scraps and fragments.

00:24:33.775 --> 00:24:36.554
When they finished, there were more

00:24:36.556 --> 00:24:39.492
than 140, including nearly all of the

00:24:39.492 --> 00:24:42.577
famous names: Stegosaurus, Triceratops,

00:24:42.579 --> 00:24:45.820
Diplodocus, Allosaurus, the list goes on.

00:24:46.919 --> 00:24:49.488
They filled the great museums of America.

00:24:49.709 --> 00:24:52.446
They turned palaeontology into a proper,

00:24:52.448 --> 00:24:54.977
professional science, and they turned

00:24:54.990 --> 00:24:57.586
the dinosaur into a public obsession,

00:24:57.588 --> 00:24:59.901
which it has remained ever since.

00:25:00.694 --> 00:25:03.249
Indeed, it became famous enough that,

00:25:03.251 --> 00:25:05.665
a century later, television writers

00:25:05.961 --> 00:25:08.429
could put a palaeontologist on a

00:25:08.431 --> 00:25:11.051
sofa in a New York coffee shop and

00:25:11.051 --> 00:25:12.609
everyone would know what that meant.

00:25:13.586 --> 00:25:16.481
And over the decades that followed, one

00:25:16.483 --> 00:25:19.154
particular picture of dinosaurs came

00:25:19.154 --> 00:25:22.626
to dominate: giant, slow, cold-blooded,

00:25:22.628 --> 00:25:25.920
stupid reptiles, dragging their tails

00:25:26.227 --> 00:25:28.521
through the swamps of a vanished world.

00:25:29.127 --> 00:25:32.017
That picture went into every museum and

00:25:32.019 --> 00:25:35.129
every textbook for the next hundred years.

00:25:35.827 --> 00:25:38.687
And it was almost completely wrong.

00:25:39.385 --> 00:25:42.097
But that is the story of part two.

00:25:43.729 --> 00:25:45.694
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:25:45.696 --> 00:25:47.597
episode — the first part of our

00:25:47.787 --> 00:25:50.378
three-part mini-series on dinosaurs.

00:25:50.636 --> 00:25:53.141
Next up we'll be moving into the story

00:25:53.143 --> 00:25:55.646
of how science got dinosaurs wrong for

00:25:55.646 --> 00:25:58.221
a hundred years, and the discovery of

00:25:58.223 --> 00:26:00.866
a single claw that changed everything.

00:26:01.432 --> 00:26:03.370
And in part three, we'll go

00:26:03.372 --> 00:26:05.666
back sixty-six million years and

00:26:05.666 --> 00:26:08.140
follow, hour by hour, what happened

00:26:08.142 --> 00:26:10.261
on the day the dinosaurs died.

00:26:11.766 --> 00:26:14.200
You've been listening to English Learning

00:26:14.202 --> 00:26:16.456
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:26:16.750 --> 00:26:19.504
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:26:19.506 --> 00:26:22.040
I'll catch you in the next episode.
